#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543836 :question/problem "![](images/1f/1f308d1a3ecf3bd0090e22c4f70389311db4db3ff06f7038db0cf7770a8837c7.png)\n\nSuppose that $F$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=- \\frac{7}{2},\\,{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=- \\frac{14}{3},\\,{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{20}{3},\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ are oriented clockwise and ${C}_{3}$ is oriented counterclockwise, calculate the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/2b/2bed1b367cd101e0e90674c619ad87da8c90744df570eeea4d9b0333ccee0de1.png)\n\nNotice that the positive orientations of the outer curve ${C}_{1}$ and the inner curve ${C}_{3}$ are opposite those stated in the question.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =-\\left(- \\frac{7}{2}\\right)+\\left(- \\frac{14}{3}\\right)-\\frac{20}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(-21)+(-28)-40}{6} \\\\  & =- \\frac{47}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543844 :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\frac{5}{4}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\frac{2}{9}}=1?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=\\frac{5}{4}$ and ${b}^{2}=\\frac{2}{9}.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=\\frac{5}{4},$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $$\n\\displaystyle a=\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{4}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}.\n$$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=\\frac{2}{9},$ the length of the vertical radius must be $$\n\\displaystyle b=\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{9}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}=\\sqrt{5},$ and the minor axis has length $$\n\\displaystyle 2b=2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543853 :question/problem "For the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\frac{82}{9}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{70}=1,\n$$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=\\frac{82}{9}$ and ${b}^{2}=70.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=\\frac{82}{9},$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $$\n\\displaystyle a=\\sqrt{\\frac{82}{9}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{82}}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=70,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{70}.$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $b=\\sqrt{70},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=\\frac{\\sqrt{82}}{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot \\sqrt{70}=2\\sqrt{70},$ and the minor axis has length $$\n\\displaystyle 2a=2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{82}}{3}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{82}}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543855 :question/problem "For the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\frac{27}{4}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\frac{3}{16}}=1,\n$$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=\\frac{27}{4}$ and ${b}^{2}=\\frac{3}{16}.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=\\frac{27}{4},$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $$\n\\displaystyle a=\\sqrt{\\frac{27}{4}}=\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=\\frac{3}{16},$ the length of the vertical radius must be $$\n\\displaystyle b=\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{16}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}.\n$$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $a=\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}=3\\sqrt{3},$ and the minor axis has length $$\n\\displaystyle 2b=2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543858 :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\frac{5}{9}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\frac{5}{16}}=1?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=\\frac{5}{9}$ and ${b}^{2}=\\frac{5}{16}.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=\\frac{5}{9},$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $$\n\\displaystyle a=\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{9}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=\\frac{5}{16},$ the length of the vertical radius must be $$\n\\displaystyle b=\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{16}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}.\n$$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $$\n\\displaystyle 2a=2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3},\n$$ and the minor axis has length $$\n\\displaystyle 2b=2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543859 :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\frac{5}{9}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\frac{3}{4}}=1?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=\\frac{5}{9}$ and ${b}^{2}=\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=\\frac{5}{9},$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $$\n\\displaystyle a=\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{9}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=\\frac{3}{4},$ the length of the vertical radius must be $$\n\\displaystyle b=\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $b=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}=\\sqrt{3},$ and the minor axis has length $$\n\\displaystyle 2a=2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543869 :question/problem "For the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\frac{5}{4}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\frac{27}{4}}=1,\n$$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=\\frac{5}{4}$ and ${b}^{2}=\\frac{27}{4}.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=\\frac{5}{4},$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $$\n\\displaystyle a=\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{4}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}.\n$$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=\\frac{27}{4},$ the length of the vertical radius must be $$\n\\displaystyle b=\\sqrt{\\frac{27}{4}}=\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $b=\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}=3\\sqrt{3},$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}=\\sqrt{5}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543878 :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{37}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\frac{73}{9}}=1?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=37$ and ${b}^{2}=\\frac{73}{9}.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=37,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{37}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=\\frac{73}{9},$ the length of the vertical radius must be $$\n\\displaystyle b=\\sqrt{\\frac{73}{9}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{73}}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=\\sqrt{37},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=\\frac{\\sqrt{73}}{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot \\sqrt{37}=2\\sqrt{37},$ and the minor axis has length $$\n\\displaystyle 2b=2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{73}}{3}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{73}}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543886 :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{53}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\frac{13}{4}}=1?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=53$ and ${b}^{2}=\\frac{13}{4}.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=53,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{53}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=\\frac{13}{4},$ the length of the vertical radius must be $$\n\\displaystyle b=\\sqrt{\\frac{13}{4}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=\\sqrt{53},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot \\sqrt{53}=2\\sqrt{53},$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}=\\sqrt{13}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543896 :question/problem "For the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\frac{47}{9}}=1,\n$$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=16$ and ${b}^{2}=\\frac{47}{9}.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=16,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{16}=4.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=\\frac{47}{9},$ the length of the vertical radius must be $$\n\\displaystyle b=\\sqrt{\\frac{47}{9}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{47}}{3}.\n$$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $a=4,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=\\frac{\\sqrt{47}}{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 4=8,$ and the minor axis has length $$\n\\displaystyle 2b=2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{47}}{3}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{47}}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543898 :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\frac{13}{4}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{17}=1?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=\\frac{13}{4}$ and ${b}^{2}=17.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=\\frac{13}{4},$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $$\n\\displaystyle a=\\sqrt{\\frac{13}{4}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}.\n$$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=17,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{17}.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $b=\\sqrt{17},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot \\sqrt{17}=2\\sqrt{17},$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}=\\sqrt{13}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543910 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{8\\cdot\\left(\\sin^{2} \\theta\\right)+8\\cdot\\left(\\cos^{2} \\theta\\right)}{\\sin \\theta}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{8\\cdot\\left(\\sin^{2} \\theta\\right)+8\\cdot\\left(\\cos^{2} \\theta\\right)}{\\sin \\theta}=\\frac{8\\cdot(\\sin^{2} \\theta+\\cos^{2} \\theta)}{\\sin \\theta}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, and then we simplify the resulting expression as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8\\cdot(\\sin^{2} \\theta+\\cos^{2} \\theta)}{\\sin \\theta} & =\\frac{8\\cdot 1}{\\sin \\theta} \\\\ & =\\frac{8}{\\sin \\theta} \\\\ & =8\\cdot\\left(\\csc \\theta\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{8\\cdot\\left(\\sin^{2} \\theta\\right)+8\\cdot\\left(\\cos^{2} \\theta\\right)}{\\sin \\theta}=8\\cdot\\left(\\csc \\theta\\right).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543932 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{10\\cdot\\left(\\sin^{2} \\phi\\right)+10\\cdot\\left(\\cos^{2} \\phi\\right)}{\\sin \\phi}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{10\\cdot\\left(\\sin^{2} \\phi\\right)+10\\cdot\\left(\\cos^{2} \\phi\\right)}{\\sin \\phi}=\\frac{10\\cdot(\\sin^{2} \\phi+\\cos^{2} \\phi)}{\\sin \\phi}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, and then we simplify the resulting expression as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{10\\cdot(\\sin^{2} \\phi+\\cos^{2} \\phi)}{\\sin \\phi} & =\\frac{10\\cdot 1}{\\sin \\phi} \\\\ & =\\frac{10}{\\sin \\phi} \\\\ & =10\\cdot\\left(\\csc \\phi\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{10\\cdot\\left(\\sin^{2} \\phi\\right)+10\\cdot\\left(\\cos^{2} \\phi\\right)}{\\sin \\phi}=10\\cdot\\left(\\csc \\phi\\right).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543940 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{9\\cdot\\left(\\sin^{2} t\\right)+9\\cdot\\left(\\cos^{2} t\\right)}{\\sin t}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{9\\cdot\\left(\\sin^{2} t\\right)+9\\cdot\\left(\\cos^{2} t\\right)}{\\sin t}=\\frac{9\\cdot(\\sin^{2} t+\\cos^{2} t)}{\\sin t}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, and then we simplify the resulting expression as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9\\cdot(\\sin^{2} t+\\cos^{2} t)}{\\sin t} & =\\frac{9\\cdot 1}{\\sin t} \\\\ & =\\frac{9}{\\sin t} \\\\ & =9\\cdot\\left(\\csc t\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{9\\cdot\\left(\\sin^{2} t\\right)+9\\cdot\\left(\\cos^{2} t\\right)}{\\sin t}=9\\cdot\\left(\\csc t\\right).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543942 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2\\cdot\\left(\\cos^{2} \\phi\\right)+2\\cdot\\left(\\sin^{2} \\phi\\right)}{\\cos \\phi}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2\\cdot\\left(\\cos^{2} \\phi\\right)+2\\cdot\\left(\\sin^{2} \\phi\\right)}{\\cos \\phi}=\\frac{2\\cdot(\\cos^{2} \\phi+\\sin^{2} \\phi)}{\\cos \\phi}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, and then we simplify the resulting expression as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\cdot(\\cos^{2} \\phi+\\sin^{2} \\phi)}{\\cos \\phi} & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1}{\\cos \\phi} \\\\ & =\\frac{2}{\\cos \\phi} \\\\ & =2\\cdot\\left(\\sec \\phi\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{2\\cdot\\left(\\cos^{2} \\phi\\right)+2\\cdot\\left(\\sin^{2} \\phi\\right)}{\\cos \\phi}=2\\cdot\\left(\\sec \\phi\\right).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543943 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $$\n\\displaystyle 8\\cdot\\left(\\frac{{sin}^{3}⁡\\phi+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\phi{cos}^{2}⁡\\phi+1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\phi}\\right).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\phi$ from the first two terms:\n\n$$\n8\\cdot\\left(\\frac{{sin}^{3}⁡\\phi+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\phi{cos}^{2}⁡\\phi+1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\phi}\\right)=8\\cdot\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\phi({sin}^{2}⁡\\phi+{cos}^{2}⁡\\phi)+1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\phi}\\right)\n$$\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, and then we simplify the resulting expression as much as possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\cdot\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\phi({sin}^{2}⁡\\phi+{cos}^{2}⁡\\phi)+1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\phi}\\right) & =8\\cdot\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\phi+1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\phi}\\right) \\\\  & =8\\cdot\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\phi}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\phi}+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\phi}\\right) \\\\  & =8\\cdot\\left(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\phi+\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\phi\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that:\n\n$$\n8\\cdot\\left(\\frac{{sin}^{3}⁡\\phi+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\phi{cos}^{2}⁡\\phi+1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\phi}\\right)=8\\cdot\\left(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\phi+\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\phi\\right)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543947 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $$\n\\displaystyle 8\\cdot\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\phi{cos}^{2}⁡\\phi+{sin}^{3}⁡\\phi}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\phi}\\right).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the numerator:\n\n$$\n8\\cdot\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\phi{cos}^{2}⁡\\phi+{sin}^{3}⁡\\phi}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\phi}\\right)=8\\cdot\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\phi\\cdot ({cos}^{2}⁡\\phi+{sin}^{2}⁡\\phi)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\phi}\\right)\n$$\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, and then we simplify the resulting expression as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\cdot\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\phi\\cdot ({cos}^{2}⁡\\phi+{sin}^{2}⁡\\phi)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\phi}\\right) & =8\\cdot\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\phi\\cdot 1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\phi}\\right) \\\\  & =8\\cdot\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\phi}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\phi}\\right) \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\phi\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n8\\cdot\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\phi{cos}^{2}⁡\\phi+{sin}^{3}⁡\\phi}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\phi}\\right)=8\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\phi.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543955 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{4\\cdot\\left(\\cos^{2} \\theta\\right)+4\\cdot\\left(\\sin^{2} \\theta\\right)}{\\cos \\theta}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4\\cdot\\left(\\cos^{2} \\theta\\right)+4\\cdot\\left(\\sin^{2} \\theta\\right)}{\\cos \\theta}=\\frac{4\\cdot(\\cos^{2} \\theta+\\sin^{2} \\theta)}{\\cos \\theta}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, and then we simplify the resulting expression as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4\\cdot(\\cos^{2} \\theta+\\sin^{2} \\theta)}{\\cos \\theta} & =\\frac{4\\cdot 1}{\\cos \\theta} \\\\ & =\\frac{4}{\\cos \\theta} \\\\ & =4\\cdot\\left(\\sec \\theta\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{4\\cdot\\left(\\cos^{2} \\theta\\right)+4\\cdot\\left(\\sin^{2} \\theta\\right)}{\\cos \\theta}=4\\cdot\\left(\\sec \\theta\\right).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543959 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $$\n\\displaystyle 2\\cdot\\left(\\frac{{sin}^{4}⁡\\phi +{sin}^{2}⁡\\phi {cos}^{2}⁡\\phi +{sin}^{2}⁡\\phi }{{sin}^{2}⁡\\phi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\phi }\\right).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the numerator:\n\n$$\n2\\cdot\\left(\\frac{{sin}^{4}⁡\\phi +{sin}^{2}⁡\\phi {cos}^{2}⁡\\phi +{sin}^{2}⁡\\phi }{{sin}^{2}⁡\\phi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\phi }\\right)=2\\cdot\\left(\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\phi ({sin}^{2}⁡\\phi +{cos}^{2}⁡\\phi )+{sin}^{2}⁡\\phi }{{sin}^{2}⁡\\phi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\phi }\\right)\n$$\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, and then we simplify the resulting expression as much as possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot\\left(\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\phi ({sin}^{2}⁡\\phi +{cos}^{2}⁡\\phi )+{sin}^{2}⁡\\phi }{{sin}^{2}⁡\\phi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\phi }\\right) & =2\\cdot\\left(\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\phi \\cdot 1+{sin}^{2}⁡\\phi }{{sin}^{2}⁡\\phi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\phi }\\right) \\\\  & =2\\cdot\\left(\\frac{2{sin}^{2}⁡\\phi }{{sin}^{2}⁡\\phi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\phi }\\right) \\\\  & =2\\cdot\\left(\\frac{2\\cdot {sin}^{2}⁡\\phi }{{sin}^{2}⁡\\phi \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\phi }\\right) \\\\  & =2\\cdot\\left(\\frac{2\\cdot \\cancel{{sin}^{2}⁡\\phi }}{\\cancel{{sin}^{2}⁡\\phi }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\phi }\\right) \\\\  & =2\\cdot\\left(\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\phi }\\right) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\phi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n2\\cdot\\left(\\frac{{sin}^{4}⁡\\phi +{sin}^{2}⁡\\phi {cos}^{2}⁡\\phi +{sin}^{2}⁡\\phi }{{sin}^{2}⁡\\phi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\phi }\\right)=4\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\phi.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543963 :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t)=\\langle - t^{2} + t, t^{3} - t^{2}, - 2 t^{2} + 2 t\\rangle$ for $t\\in [0,1).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=\\langle - 2 z, 0, - 3 y\\rangle,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\operatorname{curl}F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=- t^{2} + t,\\quad y=t^{3} - t^{2},\\quad z=- 2 t^{2} + 2 t\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\langle - 2 z, 0, - 3 y\\rangle \\\\ & =\\langle - 2 \\left(- 2 t^{2} + 2 t\\right), 0, - 3 \\left(t^{3} - t^{2}\\right)\\rangle \\\\ & =\\langle 4 t^{2} - 4 t, 0, - 3 t^{3} + 3 t^{2}\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r\\prime(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\prime(t) & =\\langle \\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(- t^{2} + t\\right), \\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(t^{3} - t^{2}\\right), \\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(- 2 t^{2} + 2 t\\right)\\rangle \\\\ & =\\langle 1 - 2 t, 3 t^{2} - 2 t, 2 - 4 t\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot r\\prime(t) & =\\langle 4 t^{2} - 4 t, 0, - 3 t^{3} + 3 t^{2}\\rangle\\cdot \\langle 1 - 2 t, 3 t^{2} - 2 t, 2 - 4 t\\rangle \\\\ & =\\left(4 t^{2} - 4 t\\right)\\cdot \\left(1 - 2 t\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\cdot \\left(3 t^{2} - 2 t\\right)+\\left(- 3 t^{3} + 3 t^{2}\\right)\\cdot \\left(2 - 4 t\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(- 8 t^{3} + 12 t^{2} - 4 t\\right)+\\left(0\\right)+\\left(12 t^{4} - 18 t^{3} + 6 t^{2}\\right) \\\\ & =12 t^{4} - 26 t^{3} + 18 t^{2} - 4 t \\\\ & =12 t^{4} - 26 t^{3} + 18 t^{2} - 4 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\operatorname{curl}F\\cdot\\mathrm dS & =\\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm dr \\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{1} F(r(t))\\cdot r\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(12 t^{4} - 26 t^{3} + 18 t^{2} - 4 t\\right)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ & =\\left[\\left(- \\frac{13 t^{4}}{2}\\right) + \\left(- 2 t^{2}\\right) + \\left(\\frac{12 t^{5}}{5}\\right) + \\left(6 t^{3}\\right)\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\ & =\\left(\\left(- \\frac{13}{2}\\right) + \\left(-2\\right) + \\left(\\frac{12}{5}\\right) + \\left(6\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right) + \\left(0\\right) + \\left(0\\right) + \\left(0\\right)\\right) \\\\ & =- \\frac{1}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543965 :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t)=\\langle 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}, 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}, 1\\rangle$ for $t\\in [0,2 \\pi).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=\\langle y, - x, 0\\rangle,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\operatorname{curl}F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\quad y=3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\quad z=1\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\langle y, - x, 0\\rangle \\\\ & =\\langle \\left(3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right), - \\left(2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right), 0\\rangle \\\\ & =\\langle 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}, - 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}, 0\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r\\prime(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\prime(t) & =\\langle \\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right), \\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right), \\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(1\\right)\\rangle \\\\ & =\\langle - 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}, 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}, 0\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot r\\prime(t) & =\\langle 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}, - 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}, 0\\rangle\\cdot \\langle - 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}, 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}, 0\\rangle \\\\ & =\\left(3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(- 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\cdot \\left(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\cdot \\left(0\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(- 6 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(- 6 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(0\\right) \\\\ & =- 6 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} - 6 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\operatorname{curl}F\\cdot\\mathrm dS & =\\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm dr \\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi} F(r(t))\\cdot r\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\left(-6\\right)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ & =\\left[\\left(- 6 t\\right)\\right]_{0}^{2 \\pi} \\\\ & =\\left(\\left(- 12 \\pi\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)\\right) \\\\ & =- 12 \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543967 :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t)=\\langle 4 t^{3} - 4 t, 9 t^{2} + 9 t, 3 t^{3} - 3 t\\rangle$ for $t\\in [-1,0).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=\\langle - z, 0, - y\\rangle,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\operatorname{curl}F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=4 t^{3} - 4 t,\\quad y=9 t^{2} + 9 t,\\quad z=3 t^{3} - 3 t\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\langle - z, 0, - y\\rangle \\\\ & =\\langle - \\left(3 t^{3} - 3 t\\right), 0, - \\left(9 t^{2} + 9 t\\right)\\rangle \\\\ & =\\langle - 3 t^{3} + 3 t, 0, - 9 t^{2} - 9 t\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r\\prime(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\prime(t) & =\\langle \\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(4 t^{3} - 4 t\\right), \\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(9 t^{2} + 9 t\\right), \\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(3 t^{3} - 3 t\\right)\\rangle \\\\ & =\\langle 12 t^{2} - 4, 18 t + 9, 9 t^{2} - 3\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot r\\prime(t) & =\\langle - 3 t^{3} + 3 t, 0, - 9 t^{2} - 9 t\\rangle\\cdot \\langle 12 t^{2} - 4, 18 t + 9, 9 t^{2} - 3\\rangle \\\\ & =\\left(- 3 t^{3} + 3 t\\right)\\cdot \\left(12 t^{2} - 4\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\cdot \\left(18 t + 9\\right)+\\left(- 9 t^{2} - 9 t\\right)\\cdot \\left(9 t^{2} - 3\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(- 36 t^{5} + 48 t^{3} - 12 t\\right)+\\left(0\\right)+\\left(- 81 t^{4} - 81 t^{3} + 27 t^{2} + 27 t\\right) \\\\ & =- 36 t^{5} - 81 t^{4} - 33 t^{3} + 27 t^{2} + 15 t \\\\ & =- 36 t^{5} - 81 t^{4} - 33 t^{3} + 27 t^{2} + 15 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\operatorname{curl}F\\cdot\\mathrm dS & =\\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm dr \\\\ & =\\int_{-1}^{0} F(r(t))\\cdot r\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ & =\\int_{-1}^{0}\\left(- 36 t^{5} - 81 t^{4} - 33 t^{3} + 27 t^{2} + 15 t\\right)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ & =\\left[\\left(- \\frac{81 t^{5}}{5}\\right) + \\left(- 6 t^{6}\\right) + \\left(- \\frac{33 t^{4}}{4}\\right) + \\left(\\frac{15 t^{2}}{2}\\right) + \\left(9 t^{3}\\right)\\right]_{-1}^{0} \\\\ & =\\left(\\left(0\\right) + \\left(0\\right) + \\left(0\\right) + \\left(0\\right) + \\left(0\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(\\frac{81}{5}\\right) + \\left(-6\\right) + \\left(- \\frac{33}{4}\\right) + \\left(\\frac{15}{2}\\right) + \\left(-9\\right)\\right) \\\\ & =- \\frac{9}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543978 :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t)=\\langle \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}, - 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}, 1\\rangle$ for $t\\in [0,2 \\pi).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=\\langle y, - x, 0\\rangle,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\operatorname{curl}F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\quad y=- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\quad z=1\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\langle y, - x, 0\\rangle \\\\ & =\\langle \\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right), - \\left(\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right), 0\\rangle \\\\ & =\\langle - 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}, - \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}, 0\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r\\prime(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\prime(t) & =\\langle \\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right), \\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right), \\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(1\\right)\\rangle \\\\ & =\\langle - \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}, - 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}, 0\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot r\\prime(t) & =\\langle - 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}, - \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}, 0\\rangle\\cdot \\langle - \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}, - 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}, 0\\rangle \\\\ & =\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(- \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\cdot \\left(0\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(2 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(2 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(0\\right) \\\\ & =2 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\operatorname{curl}F\\cdot\\mathrm dS & =\\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm dr \\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi} F(r(t))\\cdot r\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\left(2\\right)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ & =\\left[\\left(2 t\\right)\\right]_{0}^{2 \\pi} \\\\ & =\\left(\\left(4 \\pi\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)\\right) \\\\ & =4 \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543983 :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t)=\\langle - \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}, - 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}, 1\\rangle$ for $t\\in [0,2 \\pi).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=\\langle - 3 y, 3 x, 0\\rangle,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\operatorname{curl}F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=- \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\quad y=- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\quad z=1\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\langle - 3 y, 3 x, 0\\rangle \\\\ & =\\langle - 3 \\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right), 3 \\left(- \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right), 0\\rangle \\\\ & =\\langle 6 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}, - 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}, 0\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r\\prime(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\prime(t) & =\\langle \\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(- \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right), \\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right), \\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(1\\right)\\rangle \\\\ & =\\langle \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}, - 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}, 0\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot r\\prime(t) & =\\langle 6 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}, - 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}, 0\\rangle\\cdot \\langle \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}, - 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}, 0\\rangle \\\\ & =\\left(6 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(- 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\cdot \\left(0\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(6 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(6 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(0\\right) \\\\ & =6 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + 6 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\operatorname{curl}F\\cdot\\mathrm dS & =\\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm dr \\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi} F(r(t))\\cdot r\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\left(6\\right)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ & =\\left[\\left(6 t\\right)\\right]_{0}^{2 \\pi} \\\\ & =\\left(\\left(12 \\pi\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)\\right) \\\\ & =12 \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543995 :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t)=\\langle 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}, - 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}, 1\\rangle$ for $t\\in [0,2 \\pi).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=\\langle - 3 y, 3 x, 0\\rangle,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\operatorname{curl}F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\quad y=- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\quad z=1\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\langle - 3 y, 3 x, 0\\rangle \\\\ & =\\langle - 3 \\left(- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right), 3 \\left(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right), 0\\rangle \\\\ & =\\langle 9 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}, 9 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}, 0\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r\\prime(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\prime(t) & =\\langle \\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right), \\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right), \\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(1\\right)\\rangle \\\\ & =\\langle - 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}, - 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}, 0\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot r\\prime(t) & =\\langle 9 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}, 9 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}, 0\\rangle\\cdot \\langle - 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}, - 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}, 0\\rangle \\\\ & =\\left(9 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(9 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\cdot \\left(0\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(- 27 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(- 27 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(0\\right) \\\\ & =- 27 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} - 27 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ & =-27\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\operatorname{curl}F\\cdot\\mathrm dS & =\\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm dr \\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi} F(r(t))\\cdot r\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\left(-27\\right)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ & =\\left[\\left(- 27 t\\right)\\right]_{0}^{2 \\pi} \\\\ & =\\left(\\left(- 54 \\pi\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)\\right) \\\\ & =- 54 \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544004 :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t)=\\langle \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}, - 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}, 1\\rangle$ for $t\\in [0,2 \\pi).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=\\langle 3 y, - 3 x, 0\\rangle,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n\\iint_S\\operatorname{curl}F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}S.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\cos{\\left(t \\right)},\\quad y=- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},\\quad z=1\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\langle 3 y, - 3 x, 0\\rangle \\\\ & =\\langle 3 \\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right), - 3 \\left(\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right), 0\\rangle \\\\ & =\\langle - 6 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}, - 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}, 0\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $r\\prime(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\prime(t) & =\\langle \\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right), \\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right), \\frac{\\mathrm d}{\\mathrm dt}\\left(1\\right)\\rangle \\\\ & =\\langle - \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}, - 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}, 0\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot r\\prime(t) & =\\langle - 6 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}, - 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}, 0\\rangle\\cdot \\langle - \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}, - 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}, 0\\rangle \\\\ & =\\left(- 6 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(- 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(0\\right)\\cdot \\left(0\\right) \\\\ & =\\left(6 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(6 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(0\\right) \\\\ & =6 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + 6 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\\\ & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S\\operatorname{curl}F\\cdot\\mathrm dS & =\\oint_C F\\cdot\\mathrm dr \\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi} F(r(t))\\cdot r\\prime(t)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\left(6\\right)\\,\\mathrm dt \\\\ & =\\left[\\left(6 t\\right)\\right]_{0}^{2 \\pi} \\\\ & =\\left(\\left(12 \\pi\\right)\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)\\right) \\\\ & =12 \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544015 :question/problem "Suppose $|\\,a\\,|=\\frac{8}{3}$ and the angle between vectors $a$ and $b$ is $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$ Find the scalar projection of $a$ onto $b.$\n\n${\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =|\\,a\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}cos\\,\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544016 :question/problem "Suppose $|\\,a\\,|=\\frac{8}{3}$ and the angle between vectors $a$ and $b$ is $\\theta ={120}^{∘}.$ Find the scalar projection of $a$ onto $b.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =|\\,a\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({120}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544018 :question/problem "Suppose $|\\,a\\,|=\\frac{23}{4}$ and the angle between vectors $a$ and $b$ is $\\theta ={30}^{∘}.$ Find the scalar projection of $a$ onto $b.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =|\\,a\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{23}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({30}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\frac{23}{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{23\\sqrt{3}}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544021 :question/problem "Suppose $|\\,a\\,|=\\frac{17\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ and the angle between vectors $a$ and $b$ is $\\theta ={30}^{∘}.$ Find the scalar projection of $a$ onto $b.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =|\\,a\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{17\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({30}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\frac{17\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{17\\sqrt{6}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544029 :question/problem "Suppose $|\\,a\\,|=\\frac{13\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ and the angle between vectors $a$ and $b$ is $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Find the scalar projection of $a$ onto $b.$\n\n${\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =|\\,a\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{13\\sqrt{2}}{2}cos\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{13\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{13\\sqrt{6}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544032 :question/problem "Suppose $|\\,a\\,|=\\frac{19\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ and the angle between vectors $a$ and $b$ is $\\theta ={135}^{∘}.$ Find the scalar projection of $a$ onto $b.$\n\n${\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =|\\,a\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{19\\sqrt{3}}{3}cos\\,({135}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\frac{19\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{19\\sqrt{6}}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544042 :question/problem "Suppose $|\\,a\\,|=\\frac{19}{3}$ and the angle between vectors $a$ and $b$ is $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$ Find the scalar projection of $a$ onto $b.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =|\\,a\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{3}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{19}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544051 :question/problem "Suppose $|\\,a\\,|=\\frac{20\\sqrt{2}}{3}$ and the angle between vectors $a$ and $b$ is $\\theta ={30}^{∘}.$ Find the scalar projection of $a$ onto $b.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =|\\,a\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{20\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({30}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\frac{20\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\sqrt{6}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544055 :question/problem "Suppose $|\\,a\\,|=\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}$ and the angle between vectors $a$ and $b$ is $\\theta ={150}^{∘}.$ Find the scalar projection of $a$ onto $b.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =|\\,a\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({150}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{6}}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544065 :question/problem "Suppose $|\\,a\\,|=\\frac{3}{4}$ and the angle between vectors $a$ and $b$ is $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$ Find the scalar projection of $a$ onto $b.$\n\n${\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =|\\,a\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}cos\\,\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544784 :question/problem "Let $x(\\theta )$ and $y(\\theta )$ represent the $x$- and $y$-coordinates of a point on the given curve. Write expressions for $x(\\theta )$ and $y(\\theta )$ in terms of $\\theta .$\n\n$x(\\theta )\\,=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$y(\\theta )\\,=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use \n\n> $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,$ and $\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$\n\nwhere $x,$ $y,$ and $r$ are functions of $\\theta .$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we get \n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\ny=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =6{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544815 :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-1.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y) & ={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=-1.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,0)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,0).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})j \\\\  & =(2xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}y)-2x)i+({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}y)+2y)j \\\\ ∇f(1,0) & =(2(1)(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(({1}^{2})(0))-2(1))i+((1)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(({1}^{2})(0))+2(0))j \\\\  & =-2\\,i+j \\\\  & =⟨-2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(1,0)=⟨-2,1⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(1,0).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-1}{-2} & =\\frac{y-0}{1} \\\\ x-1 & =-2y \\\\ x+2y-1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544819 :question/problem "Find $k$ given that the vectors $\\mathbf{a}=⟨1,2,k⟩$, $\\mathbf{b}=⟨-3,0,4⟩$, and $\\mathbf{c}=⟨2,-1,5⟩$ are coplanar.\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & k \\\\ -3 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{vmatrix}-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{vmatrix}+k\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(0\\cdot 5-4\\cdot (-1))-2((-3)\\cdot 5-4\\cdot 2)+k((-3)\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =4-2(-15-8)+3k \\\\  & =50+3k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =0 \\\\ 50+3k & =0 \\\\ k & =-\\frac{50}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=-\\frac{50}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544855 :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$, given the points $A(-1,1,1)$, $B(2,-2,1)$, $C(1,2,2)$, and $D(2,-1,4).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(2i-2j+k)-(-i+j+k) \\\\  & =3i-3j \\\\  & =⟨3,-3,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(i+2j+2k)-(-i+j+k) \\\\  & =2i+j+k \\\\  & =⟨2,1,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =d-a \\\\  & =(2i-j+4k)-(-i+j+k) \\\\  & =3i-2j+3k \\\\  & =⟨3,-2,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}-(-3)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(1\\cdot 3-(-2)\\cdot 1)+3(2\\cdot 3-3\\cdot 1)+0 \\\\  & =15+9 \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelpiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|24|=24.\n$$"}]}
